    "C24","C25","C26","C27","C35","C36","C39","C42","C44",
    "C59","C63","C68","C70","C72","C81"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "No supplied record directly measures the query diltiazem-type benzothiazepine absolute oral bioavailability; the numeric L1 gold records in visible contexts are all above 20% (apixaban 49–50%, tamoxifen ~100%, encainide 76–78%, midodrine 90–100%, pentopril 50–70%, cefroxadine ~100%).",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C03","C04","C05","C07","C08","C09","C10","C11","C13","C14","C16","C17","C18","C19","C20","C21","C22","C23","C24","C25","C26"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Apixaban, which shares methoxyphenyl and lactam features with the query, remains above the F=20% threshold under strong CYP3A4/P-gp induction (37–39% with rifampin) and across species (30% rat, 70–88% dog), so efflux or induction alone does not establish F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C13","C19","C20","C35","C36","C39","C42","C44"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only sub-20% values in visible records are species- or metabolite-specific tamoxifen records (horse F 13.15%; 4-OHT poor due to glucuronidation) and do not transfer to the query's human oral context.",
      "card_ids": ["C28","C31"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 records for basic amine drugs (imipramine 100% human absorption; diphenhydramine, chlorpromazine, and promethazine high Caco-2 permeability) support efficient intestinal absorption for the query's basic amine, moderate logD profile.",
      "card_ids": ["C59","C60","C62","C63","C64","C67","C68","C69"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall/efflux records are mechanistically supportive but not diltiazem-specific: compound 16a shows Papp A-B 19.9×10^-6 cm/s with ER 0.7, promethazine shows passive non-directional transport (fold difference 1.1), and imipramine shows high Caco-2 permeability; C70/C73 and C72/C74/C80 are duplicate records counted once. The tamoxifen grapefruit-juice record is not threshold-relevant because tamoxifen F is ~100% despite P-gp/CYP3A4 involvement.",
      "card_ids": ["C70","C73","C72","C74","C80","C81","C71","C03","C04","C07"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C70","C71","C72","C73","C74","C75","C76","C77","C78","C79","C80","C81"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct diltiazem-specific absolute F measurement is present in any supplied record.",
    "No diltiazem-specific gut-wall efflux or intestinal metabolism assay is present; L5 records are for other scaffolds or formulations.",
    "L3 AUC/Cmax values lack an IV comparator and cannot yield absolute bioavailability.",
    "First-pass ester hydrolysis and CYP3A4/P-gp contributions for the query are not directly quantified.",
    "Transfer from apixaban, tamoxifen, and basic amine records remains analog-based."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L5 gut-wall efflux/intestinal metabolism evidence is moderately applicable and supportive: the most relevant records show high absorptive permeability and no significant efflux for similar basic amines, while no affirmative threshold-relevant low-F evidence for the query appears. The high prediction is retained and modestly strengthened; confidence remains moderate."
}

